---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Research Data and Sources"
  description="See the data sources, derived datasets, reproducibility notes and limitations used in Betting Maths original research."
>
  <section class="content-card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research transparency</p>
    <h1>Research Data and Sources</h1>
    <p class="lead">Betting Maths publishes the inputs, derived data and calculation methods behind its original research wherever practical, so readers can inspect what was measured and reproduce the arithmetic.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Principle:</strong> observed market data is labelled as observed data; hypothetical examples are labelled as models. We do not mix the two.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card article-section">
    <h2>Current public datasets</h2>
    <div class="dataset-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Premier League 2025/26</p>
      <h3>Opening-weekend 1X2 margins</h3>
      <p>Ten Premier League matches with Bet365, market-average and best-available opening and closing 1X2 prices, plus Betting Maths-derived book percentages.</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Underlying source</dt><dd>Football-Data.co.uk, 2025/26 English Premier League CSV (E0)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Sample</dt><dd>All 10 matches in the opening Premier League round, 15–18 August 2025</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Derived fields</dt><dd>Opening book %, closing book %, and best-available combined book %</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Published by Betting Maths</dt><dd>20 August 2026</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button primary" href="/data/premier-league-opening-weekend-2025-26-1x2-margins.csv">Download CSV</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/analysis/premier-league-opening-weekend-overround-2025-26/">Read the margin study</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card article-section">
    <h2>How derived data is created</h2>
    <p>When Betting Maths uses an external dataset, we try to preserve a clear distinction between the source fields and the figures we calculate ourselves.</p>
    <ul class="standard-list">
      <li>Source odds remain identifiable in the downloadable file.</li>
      <li>Derived book percentages are calculated from the stated formula.</li>
      <li>Rounding is applied only for presentation; the methodology page explains the general approach.</li>
      <li>Sample size and date range are stated on the research page.</li>
      <li>Important limitations are published alongside the findings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card article-section">
    <h2>Primary and secondary sources</h2>
    <p>For mathematical research, the strongest source is often the underlying data itself. For rules, definitions or regulatory claims, Betting Maths prefers primary sources such as official bookmaker/exchange rules, GOV.UK, the Gambling Commission or the organisation responsible for the rule being described.</p>
    <p>Secondary sources may be used for context, but they should not replace an available primary source for an important factual claim.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card article-section">
    <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
    <p>A reader should be able to take the published inputs and get materially the same answer. That does not mean every page needs a downloadable spreadsheet, but original research should disclose enough information to test the result.</p>
    <div class="formula-box">
      <p><strong>Example:</strong> for decimal odds 2.00, 3.50 and 4.00:</p>
      <p>Book % = (1/2.00 + 1/3.50 + 1/4.00) × 100 = 103.57%</p>
    </div>
    <p>If a future analysis depends on modelling assumptions rather than directly observed data, those assumptions will be stated explicitly.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card article-section">
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>If a derived dataset contains a transcription or calculation error, we will correct the public file and the associated article. Material corrections should be noted on the affected page.</p>
    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Report an error</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/methodology/">Calculation Methodology</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial Standards</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .article-section { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
    .dataset-card { padding: 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 14px; background: white; }
    .dataset-card dl { display: grid; gap: 0.65rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
    .dataset-card dl div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 180px 1fr; gap: 0.75rem; padding-bottom: 0.55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .dataset-card dt { font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-navy); }
    .dataset-card dd { margin: 0; color: var(--color-muted); }
    .standard-list { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
    .formula-box { padding: 1rem 1.15rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 14px; background: var(--color-bg); }
    .formula-box p { margin: 0.4rem 0; }
    @media (max-width: 700px) { .dataset-card dl div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; } }
  </style>
</MainLayout>
